== Complete list of races ==

=== Alabama ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Alabama, 2014}}
Three-term incumbent Republican [[Jeff Sessions]] was re-elected with 63% of the vote in 2008.<ref name="BostonGlobe">{{cite news | url=http://www.boston.com/news/politics/2008/election_results/us_senate | title =Campaign 08 US Senate results | newspaper=[[The Boston Globe]] | publisher=boston.com | date =November 4, 2008 | deadurl=yes}} {{Dead link|date=April 2014|bot=RjwilmsiBot}}</ref> He will be 67 years old in 2014. Sessions is seeking re-election. No Democrat filed to run against him.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://blog.al.com/wire/2014/02/democrats_pick_up_a_handful_of.html#incart_river|title=Democrats pick up a handful of candidates; governor only contested statewide race in primary|work=AL.com|date=February 7, 2014|accessdate=February 23, 2014|first=Mike|last=Cason}}</ref>

=== Alaska ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Alaska, 2014}}
One-term incumbent Democrat [[Mark Begich]] was elected with 48% of the vote in 2008, defeating six-term Senator [[Ted Stevens]] by 3,953 votes (a margin of 1.25 percent).<ref name="BostonGlobe"/> Begich will be 52 years old in 2014 and is seeking re-election to a second term.<ref name=stevens>{{cite news | url=http://www.adn.com/ted-stevens/story/753646.html | title=Stevens files candidacy for 2014 election | newspaper=Anchorage Daily News | date=April 8, 2009}}</ref> Stevens, who would have been almost 91 years old at the time of the election, had already filed for a rematch back in 2009,<ref name=stevens/> but was [[2010 Alaska Turbo Otter crash|killed in a plane crash]] the following year.

Republican Lieutenant Governor [[Mead Treadwell]],<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.alaskadispatch.com/article/alaska-lieutenant-governor-exploring-us-senate-run-2014 | title=Alaska lieutenant governor exploring US Senate run in 2014 | newspaper=Alaska Dispatch | accessdate=May 24, 2013 | last=Adams | first=Eric Christopher | date= December 1, 2012}}</ref> [[United States Senate election in Alaska, 2010|2010]] nominee [[Joe Miller (Alaska politician)|Joe Miller]],<ref>{{cite news | last=Burns | first=Alexander | title=Joe Miller files papers for Senate | url=http://www.politico.com/story/2013/05/joe-miller-alaska-senate-2014-91960.html | newspaper=Politico | date=May 28, 2013}}</ref> State Natural Resources Commissioner [[Daniel S. Sullivan]],<ref name=Sullivan>{{cite news|last=Trygstad|first=Kyle|title=Alaska GOP Primary Set to Expand #AKSEN|url=http://atr.rollcall.com/alaska-gop-primary-set-to-expand/|accessdate=September 14, 2013|newspaper=Roll Call|date=September 12, 2013}}</ref> and Air Force veteran John Jaramillo ran for the GOP nomination.  In the August 19 primary, Sullivan won the Republican nomination with 40% and faces Begich in the general election.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.wggb.com/2014/08/20/sullivan-beats-tea-party-in-alaska-gop-senate-race/|title=Sullivan beats tea party in Alaska GOP Senate race|work=WGGB Springfield|accessdate=October 27, 2014}}</ref>

=== Arkansas ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Arkansas, 2014}}
Two-term incumbent Democrat [[Mark Pryor]] was re-elected with 80% of the vote without Republican opposition in 2008.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://politicalticker.blogs.cnn.com/2013/05/31/dem-pryor-no-one-tells-me-what-to-do | title =Dem. Pryor: No one tells me what to do | publisher=cnn.com | date =May 31, 2013}}</ref> He will be 51 years old in 2014. Pryor is running for a third term.<ref name=pryor>{{cite news | url=http://www.arktimes.com/ArkansasBlog/archives/2012/04/19/sen-mark-pryor-is-running-for-re-election-in-2014 | title=Sen. Mark Pryor is running for re-election in 2014 | publisher=Arkansas Times | date=April 19, 2012}}</ref>

Freshman Representative [[Tom Cotton]] of [[Arkansas's 4th congressional district]] is the Republican nominee.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.politico.com/2014-election/results/map/senate/arkansas/#.U5HrtfldV1Y | title =2014 Arkansas Senate Primaries Results | publisher =politico.com | date =June 5, 2014}}</ref>

=== Colorado ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Colorado, 2014}}
One-term incumbent Democrat [[Mark Udall]] was elected with 53% of the vote in 2008. He will be 64 years old in 2014. Udall is running for re-election.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://thehill.com/blogs/ballot-box/fundraising/279613-dem-senators-form-joint-fundraising-committee | title =Dem senators form joint fundraising committee | first =Megan R. | last= Wilson | publisher=thehill.com | date =January 28, 2013}}</ref>

Congressman [[Cory Gardner]] of [[Colorado's 4th congressional district]] is the Republican nominee; his late entry into the race caused numerous Republicans to withdraw their candidacies.<ref name=Gardner>{{cite news|last=Johnson|first=Hugh|title=Mark Aspiri becomes the 5th Republican to drop out of the U.S. Senate race since Rep. Cory Gardner jumped in|url=http://blogs.denverpost.com/thespot/2014/04/01/mark-aspiri-suspends-u-s-senate-bid/107680/|accessdate=April 3, 2014|newspaper=Denver Post|date=April 1, 2014}}</ref>

Gaylon Kent, 48 of Steamboat Springs, is the Libertarian Party's nominee.

[[Unity Party of America]] founder and National Chairman Bill Hammons is the Unity Party nominee.

=== Delaware ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Delaware, 2014}}
Democrat [[Chris Coons]] won in the [[United States Senate election in Delaware, 2010|2010 special election]] caused by [[Joe Biden]]'s election as [[Vice President of the United States|Vice President]], winning by a 57% to 41% margin. Coons will be 51 years old in 2014. Coons is seeking re-election.  His Republican opponent is engineer Kevin Wade.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.therepublic.com/view/story/2389184ee33041d5b9dfb028579b934b/DE--Delaware-Primary|archiveurl=https://wayback.archive.org/web/20140917022044/http://www.therepublic.com/view/story/2389184ee33041d5b9dfb028579b934b/DE--Delaware-Primary|archivedate=2014-09-17|title=Wade, Simpler win Republican primaries in Delaware, Mayrack gets Democratic nod for auditor}}</ref>

=== Georgia ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Georgia, 2014}}
Two-term incumbent Republican [[Saxby Chambliss]] was re-elected with 57% of the vote in 2008 in a [[runoff election]] with former state Representative [[Jim Martin (Georgia politician)|Jim Martin]]; Georgia requires run-off elections when no Senate candidate wins over 50% of the vote. Chambliss is not seeking a third term.<ref name=chambliss>{{cite news | url=http://thehill.com/blogs/ballot-box/senate-races/279287-report-sen-saxby-chambliss-to-retire| title=Report: Sen. Saxby Chambliss to retire | date=January 25, 2013 | publisher=The Hill}}</ref>

Political activist Derrick Grayson,<ref>{{cite web | url=http://clatl.com/freshloaf/archives/2013/05/23/meet-derrick-grayson-the-minister-of-truth-yet-another-us-senate-candidate | title=Meet Derrick Grayson, 'The Minister of Truth,' yet another U.S. Senate candidate | date=May 23, 2013 | accessdate=May 23, 2013 | work=[[Creative Loafing (Atlanta)]] | first=Max | last=Blau}}</ref> Representatives [[Jack Kingston]] of [[Georgia's 1st congressional district]],<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.usatoday.com/story/onpolitics/2013/05/02/kingston-georgia-senate-broun/2130485 | title =Kingston joins crowded GOP field for Ga. Senate | agency=Associated Press | publisher=usatoday.com | date =May 2, 2013 | first1=Catalina | last1=Camia}}</ref> [[Paul Broun]] of [[Georgia's 10th congressional district]],<ref>{{cite web | url=http://chronicle.augusta.com/news/government/2013-02-06/paul-broun-enters-2014-senate-race-succeed-saxby-chambliss | title =Paul Broun enters 2014 Senate race to succeed Saxby Chambliss | first=Bill | last=Barrow | author2 =[[Associated Press]] | publisher=[[The Augusta Chronicle]] | date =February 6, 2013}}</ref> and [[Phil Gingrey]] of [[Georgia's 11th congressional district]]<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.ajc.com/weblogs/political-insider/2013/mar/27/phil-gingrey-joins-2014-contest-us-senate | title =Phil Gingrey joins 2014 contest for U.S. Senate | first=Jim | last=Galloway | publisher=[[Atlanta Journal-Constitution]]'' | date =March 27, 2013}}</ref> all declared their candidacy for the Republican nomination, as did former Secretary of State [[Karen Handel]]<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.usatoday.com/story/onpolitics/2013/05/17/karen-handel-senate-georgia-republican/2193343 | title =Ex-Komen exec Karen Handel declares Ga. Senate bid | first=Catalina | last=Camila | publisher=usatoday.com | date =May 17, 2013}}</ref> and wealthy businessman [[David Perdue]], cousin of former Governor [[Sonny Perdue]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.newsmax.com/Politics/georgia-poll-senate-/2014/03/20/id/560871/ |title=Poll: Two Runoffs Likely in Race to Succeed Ga. Sen. Chambliss |publisher=Newsmax.com |date= |accessdate=June 27, 2014}}</ref> In the May 20 primary, no candidate received a majority of votes, so the top two candidates faced each other in a runoff; Perdue won against Kingston in the runoff primary election on July 22 with 50.9% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.politico.com/story/2014/07/georgia-election-2014-michelle-nunn-david-perdue-jack-kingston-109252.html |title=Perdue wins Georgia Senate runoff |publisher=http://www.politico.com|date=July 22, 2014 |accessdate=July 23, 2014}}</ref>

[[Michelle Nunn]], CEO of [[Points of Light]] and the daughter of former U.S. Senator [[Sam Nunn]], won the Democratic nomination.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.timesfreepress.com/news/2014/may/20/georgia-sen-michelle-nunn-wins-democratic-primary-/|title=In Georgia, Sen. Michelle Nunn wins Democratic primary; GOP race close|agency=Associated Press|date=May 20, 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.mcclatchydc.com/2013/08/08/198877/michelle-nunn-daughter-of-sam.html#.UgwXF5KTgV0 | title =Michelle Nunn, daughter of Sam Nunn, seeks U.S. Senate seat | author =Mike Owen | author2 =''[[McClatchy News Service]]'' | publisher =mcclatchydc.com | date =August 8, 2013}}</ref> Other declared Democratic candidates included former State Senator [[Steen Miles]], psychiatrist Branko Radulovacki, and former US Army Ranger Todd Robinson.

Amanda Swafford, a former [[Flowery Branch, Georgia]] city councilwoman, received the [[Libertarian Party of Georgia]] nomination.

=== Hawaii (special) ===
{{main|United States Senate special election in Hawaii, 2014}}
[[Daniel Inouye]], the [[List of members of the United States Congress by longevity of service|second longest serving]] United States Senator in U.S. history, died on December 17, 2012, after respiratory complications.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://abcnews.go.com/Politics/wireStory/sen-daniel-inouye-hawaii-dead-88-18001608#.UM-neKU1ZSU | title=Sen. Daniel Inouye of Hawaii Dead at 88 | date=December 17, 2012}}</ref> [[Hawaii]] law allows [[Neil Abercrombie]], the [[Governor of Hawaii]], to appoint an interim Senator "who serves until the next regularly-scheduled general election, chosen from a list of three prospective appointees that the prior incumbent's political party submits."<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.cga.ct.gov/2009/rpt/2009-R-0018.htm | title=LAWS GOVERNING U.S. SENATE VACANCIES | date=January 21, 2009 | accessdate=December 17, 2012}}</ref> Abercrombie picked his Lieutenant Governor, [[Brian Schatz]], to fill the Senate seat.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/27/us/politics/lt-gov-brian-schatz-of-hawaii-to-succeed-inouye-in-senate.html?_r=0 | title =Hawaii’s Lieutenant Governor to Succeed Inouye | first=Jeremy S.| last=Peters | publisher=nytimes.com | date =December 26, 2012}}</ref> Inouye was re-elected in 2010 with 72 percent of the vote.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://hawaii.gov/elections/results/2010/general/files/histatewide.pdf | title=GENERAL ELECTION – State of Hawaii – Statewide | date=November 16, 2012 | accessdate=December 17, 2012}}</ref> Schatz was challenged in the Democratic primary by Congresswoman [[Colleen Hanabusa]] of [[Hawaii's 1st congressional district]], who Inouye had hoped would be his successor.<ref>{{cite news | title=Hanabusa will challenge Schatz in Hawaii Senate primary | first=Aaron | last=Blake | date=April 23, 2013 | publisher=The Washington Post | url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/post-politics/wp/2013/04/23/hanabusa-will-challenge-schatz-in-hawaii-senate-primary}}</ref>  Schatz defeated Hanabusa in the primary with 48.5% to 47.8%.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.civilbeat.com/2014/08/hawaii-primary-schatz-hangs-on-to-win-us-senate-race/|title=Hawaii Primary: Schatz Hangs On To Win US Senate Race|work=Civil Beat|accessdate=October 27, 2014}}</ref>

[[Campbell Cavasso]], former State Representative and nominee for the U.S. Senate in [[United States Senate election in Hawaii, 2004|2004]] and [[United States Senate election in Hawaii, 2010|2010]], is the Republican nominee.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.civilbeat.com/articles/2013/10/22/20203-civil-beat-poll-schatz-has-narrow-lead-over-hanabusa/|title=Civil Beat Poll — Schatz Has Narrow Lead Over Hanabusa|publisher=Honolulu Civil Beat|first=Chad|last=Blair|date=October 22, 2013|accessdate=October 23, 2013}}</ref>

=== Idaho ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Idaho, 2014}}
One-term incumbent Republican [[Jim Risch]] was elected with 58% of the vote in 2008. He will be 71 years old in 2014. Risch is seeking a second term.<ref name=risch>{{cite news | newspaper=Idaho Statesman | url=http://voices.idahostatesman.com/2012/04/13/idahopolitics/prepping_2014_reelection_idaho_sen_jim_risch_holding_capitol_hil | title=Prepping for 2014 reelection bid, Idaho Sen. Jim Risch holding Capitol Hill fundraiser next week | publisher=IdahoStatesman.com | accessdate=November 24, 2012}}</ref>

Boise attorney [[Nels Mitchell]] is the Democratic nominee.<ref>{{cite news | newspaper=Idaho Statesman | url=http://www.idahostatesman.com/2014/01/13/2970838/boise-attorney-a-political-newcomer.html | title=Boise attorney, a political newcomer and Democrat, to take on Sen. Jim Risch | publisher=IdahoStatesman.com | accessdate=March 13, 2012}}</ref>

=== Illinois ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Illinois, 2014}}
Three-term incumbent and [[Assistant party leaders of the United States Senate|Senate Majority Whip]] Democrat [[Dick Durbin]] was re-elected with 68% of the vote in 2008. He will be 70 years old in 2014. Durbin is running for a fourth term.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://atr.rollcall.com/illinois-durbin-will-run-for-re-election | title=Illinois: Durbin Will Run for Re-Election | first1=Abby | last1=Livingston | first2=Meredith | last2=Shiner | date=March 8, 2013 | accessdate=June 26, 2013 | publisher=rollcall.com | newspaper=Roll Call}}</ref>

State Senator [[Jim Oberweis]] is the Republican nominee.<ref name=Oberweis>{{cite news|last=Skiba|first=Katherine|title=Oberweis faces tough race against Durbin|url=http://articles.chicagotribune.com/2014-03-19/news/chi-illinois-republican-senate-20140318_1_republican-jim-oberweis-doug-truax-state-senate-seat|accessdate=April 3, 2014|newspaper=Chicago Tribune|date=March 19, 2014}}</ref> He defeated primary challenger Doug Truax with 56% of the vote.

=== Iowa ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Iowa, 2014}}
Five-term incumbent Democrat [[Tom Harkin]] was re-elected with 63% of the vote in 2008. Harkin announced on January 26, 2013 that he would not seek a sixth term.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.csmonitor.com/USA/Latest-News-Wires/2013/0126/Senator-Tom-Harkin-Democrat-from-Iowa-will-not-seek-reelection | title =Senator Tom Harkin, Democrat from Iowa, will not seek reelection | author =''[[Reuters]]'' | publisher =csmonitor.com | date =January 26, 2013}}</ref> Congressman [[Bruce Braley]] is the Democratic nominee.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.nationaljournal.com/blogs/hotlineoncall/2013/04/braley-has-1-million-on-hand-as-republicans-seek-a-senate-candidate-15 | title =Braley Has $1 Million On Hand, As Republicans Seek A Senate Candidate | accessdate=June 26, 2013 | first=Alex | last=Brown | publisher=nationaljournal.com | date =April 15, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://sos.iowa.gov/elections/electioninfo/primary/index.html |title=Primary Election |accessdate=March 21, 2014}}</ref>

State Senator [[Joni Ernst]] is the Republican nominee.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.desmoinesregister.com/story/opinion/columnists/kathie-obradovich/2014/06/03/iowa-senate-joni-ernst-turns-aim-to-bruce-braley/9941807/ |title=Iowa Senate: Jodi Ernst turns aim to Bruce Braley |accessdate=June 4, 2014}}</ref>

[[United States Senate election in Iowa, 2014#Libertarian candidate|Doug Butzier]], who was the Libertarian Party's nominee, died in a plane crash on October 13, 2014 but will still appear on the ballot.<ref>{{cite news |author=<!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.--> |title=Local doctor running for U.S. Senate killed in plane crash |url=http://www.thonline.com/news/breaking/article_fea46b32-53ad-11e4-882d-001a4bcf6878.html |newspaper=Dubuque Telegraph Herald |date=October 14, 2014 |accessdate=October 14, 2014}}</ref>

=== Kansas ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Kansas, 2014}}
Three-term incumbent Republican [[Pat Roberts]] was re-elected with 60% of the vote in 2008. He will be 78 years old in 2014. Roberts is seeking a fourth term.<ref name=roberts>{{cite news | url=http://www.hutchnews.com/Localregional/roberts2009-10-31T20-49-31 | first= Mary | last=Clarkin | title=Roberts already planning 2014 bid | date=October 31, 2009 | accessdate=June 26, 2013 | publisher=hutchnews.com | newspaper=The Hutchinson News}}</ref>  He faced a primary challenge from radiologist Milton Wolf, a conservative [[Tea Party movement|Tea Party]] supporter and distant cousin of President [[Barack Obama]].<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.huffingtonpost.com/2013/10/09/milton-wolf-us-senate_n_4067803.html | work=Huffington Post | first=Ashley | last=Alman | title=Tea Party Activist To Challenge Roberts In 2014 | date=October 9, 2013}}</ref>  Roberts defeated Wolf in the Republican primary by 48% to 41%, far less than expected.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/the-fix/wp/2014/08/07/pat-roberts-won-the-kansas-primary-on-tuesday-one-fix-reader-called-it/|title=Pat Roberts won the Kansas primary on Tuesday. One Fix reader called it.|work=Washington Post|accessdate=October 27, 2014}}</ref>  [[Shawnee County, Kansas|Shawnee County]] District Attorney [[Chad Taylor (Kansas)|Chad Taylor]] won the Democratic nomination.<ref name=Taylor>{{cite news|last=Haake|first=Garrett|title=Kansas Senate race heats up with new entry and endorsements|url=http://www.kshb.com/news/political/kansas-senate-race-heats-up-with-new-entry-and-endorsements|accessdate=February 28, 2014|newspaper=KSHB 41|date=February 27, 2014}}</ref> Randall Batson from Wichita will be on the general election ballot as a Libertarian.<ref>{{cite web|last=McMillin |first=Molly |url=http://www.kansas.com/2014/04/26/3425042/kansas-libertarians-choose-election.html |title=Kansas Libertarians choose election slate &#124; Wichita Eagle |publisher=Kansas.com |date= |accessdate=June 27, 2014}}</ref> Additionally, [[Greg Orman]] qualified for the ballot as an [[Independent (politician)|independent]].<ref name=hancock>{{cite news|last1=Hancock|first1=Peter|title=Senate challenger says he has enough signatures to get on ballot as independent candidate|url=http://www2.ljworld.com/news/2014/jul/28/senate-challenger-says-he-has-enough-signatures-ge/|work=Lawrence Journal-World|date=July 28, 2014}}</ref>

On September 3, Taylor announced that he was dropping out of the election, leading to speculation that Democrats would support Orman's candidacy.<ref name="Washington Post">{{cite web|url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/post-politics/wp/2014/09/03/democratic-nominee-chad-taylor-drops-out-of-kansas-senate-race/|title=Democratic nominee Chad Taylor drops out of Kansas Senate race|work=Washington Post|accessdate=October 27, 2014}}</ref> On September 18, the [[Kansas Supreme Court]] ruled that Taylor's name had to be removed from the ballot.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.nytimes.com/2014/09/19/us/kansas-democrats-withdrawal-from-senate-race-is-upheld.html?ref=politics|title=Kansas Justices Back Senate Candidate’s Withdrawal |author=Mitch Smith|date=September 18, 2014 |publisher=The New York Times}}</ref> Orman has not committed to caucusing with either party.<ref name="Washington Post"/>

=== Kentucky ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Kentucky, 2014}}
Five-term Republican incumbent and [[Party leaders of the United States Senate|Senate Minority Leader]] [[Mitch McConnell]] was re-elected with 53% of the vote in 2008. He will be 72 years old in 2014. McConnell is seeking re-election to a sixth term.<ref name=mcconnell>{{cite news | url=http://www.politico.com/news/stories/1210/46642_Page2.html | publisher=Politico.com | work=[[Politico]] | title=Mitch McConnell plans power plays against Democrats | date=December 20, 2010}}</ref> McConnell defeated businessman [[Matt Bevin]] in the Republican primary on May 20.<ref name=kyprim>{{cite web | url=http://www.courier-journal.com/story/news/politics/elections/kentucky/2014/05/20/ap-mcconnell-grimes-claim-senate-primaries/9350463/| title =McConnell crushes Bevin; Grimes cruises to win | publisher =Courier-Journal | date =May 21, 2014}}</ref>

[[Kentucky]] [[Secretary of State of Kentucky|Secretary of State]] [[Alison Lundergan Grimes]], with support from much of Kentucky's Democratic leadership, won the Democratic primary.<ref name=kyprim/><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.courier-journal.com/article/20130730/NEWS010605/307300087/Alison-Lundergan-Grimes-blasts-Mitch-McConnell-campaign-rollout | title =Alison Lundergan Grimes blasts Mitch McConnell at campaign rollout | author =Joseph Gerth | publisher =courier-journal.com | date =July 31, 2013}}</ref>  Actress [[Ashley Judd]] considered running for the Democratic nomination, but ultimately decided against it.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/27/ashley-judd-passes-on-senate-run-in-kentucky/?_r=0 | title =Ashley Judd Passes on Senate Run in Kentucky | author =Trip Gabriel | publisher =nytimes.com | date =March 27, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.politico.com/story/2012/12/ashley-judd-exploring-senate-run-84542.html | title =Ashley Judd exploring Senate run | author =Manu Raju | publisher =politico.com | date =December 4, 2012}}</ref>

Ed Marksberry is pursuing an independent bid after dropping out of the Democratic field in September 2013.<ref name="wfpl.org">{{cite web | url=http://wfpl.org/post/senate-candidates-decry-kentucky-democratic-party-e-mail-promoting-alison-lundergan-grimes | title=Senate Candidates Decry Kentucky Democratic Party E-mail Promoting Alison Lundergan Grimes | last1=Bailey | first1=Phillip | date=July 5, 2013 | accessdate=November 29, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://mycn2.com/politics/ed-marksberry-to-drop-out-of-democratic-us-senate-primary-to-run-as-independent |title=Ed Marksberry to drop out of Democratic U.S. Senate primary to run as independent |last1=Alessi |first1=Ryan |date=September 23, 2013 |website=mycn2.com |accessdate=February 22, 2014}}</ref>

=== Louisiana ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Louisiana, 2014}}
Three-term incumbent Democrat [[Mary Landrieu]] was re-elected with 52% of the vote in 2008.  Landrieu is running for a fourth term.<ref name=landrieu>{{cite news | url =http://www.nola.com/politics/index.ssf/2011/02/sen_mary_landrieu_plans_to_see.html | title=Sen. Mary Landrieu is seeking a fourth Senate term | date=February 5, 2011 | newspaper=The Times-Picayune}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | first=Rusty | last=Costanza | url=http://www.nola.com/politics/index.ssf/2011/07/sen_mary_landrieu_is_in_tenuou.html | title=Sen. Mary Landrieu is in a tenuous position as the last statewide-elected Democrat in Louisiana | publisher=NOLA.com | date= | accessdate=November 24, 2012}}</ref>

Louisiana uses a unique [[jungle primary]] system that eschews primaries in favor of [[run-off election]]s between the top two candidates; this run-off can be avoided if the winning candidate receives over 50% of the vote. Democrats Wayne Ables, Vallian Senegal, and William Waymire are running against Landrieu in the election, as are Republicans [[Bill Cassidy]] (representative of [[Louisiana's 6th congressional district]]), Thomas Clements (small business owner), and retired [[United States Air Force|Air Force]] [[Colonel]] Rob Maness.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/post-politics/wp/2013/04/02/rep-bill-cassidy-announces-senate-bid/ | title =Rep. Bill Cassidy announces Senate bid | author =Rachel Weiner | publisher =washingtonpost.com | date =April 2, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.nola.com/politics/index.ssf/2013/05/us_senate_race_attracts_air_fo.html | title =Sen. Mary Landrieu's new challenger: Air Force veteran from Madisonville | author =Drew Broach | publisher =nola.com | date =May 13, 2013}}</ref> [[Electrical Engineer]] Brannon McMorris is running as a Libertarian.<ref name="US Elections~Louisiana"/>

=== Maine ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Maine, 2014}}
Three-term incumbent Republican [[Susan Collins]] is seeking a fourth term.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2013/03/20/susan-collins-senate-2014-election-maine/2003837 | title=Moderate GOP Sen. Collins intends to run again; USA Today | publisher=usatoday.com | date=March 20, 2013 | accessdate=April 3, 2013 | first1=Catalina | last1=Camia}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.kjonline.com/news/growing-influencesen_-susan-collins-has-becomea-key-player-in-congress_2010-08-28.html | title=Sen. Susan Collins has become a key player in Congress &#124; The Kennebec Journal, Augusta, ME | publisher=Kjonline.com | date=August 29, 2010 | accessdate=November 24, 2012}}</ref> [[Shenna Bellows]], former Executive Director of the [[American Civil Liberties Union]] of Maine, is the Democratic nominee.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.kjonline.com/politics/Shenna_Bellows_to_launch_U_S__Senate_campaing_on_Oct__23_.html|title=Shenna Bellows to launch U.S. Senate campaign on Oct. 23 |publisher=[[Kennebec Journal]] |date=October 8, 2013 |accessdate=October 8, 2013}}</ref>

=== Massachusetts ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Massachusetts, 2014}}
{{see also|United States Senate special election in Massachusetts, 2013}}
Five-term incumbent and 2004 Democratic presidential nominee [[John Kerry]] was re-elected with 66% of the vote in 2008. Kerry resigned in early 2013 to become [[U.S. Secretary of State]].<ref>{{cite news | url=http://swampland.time.com/2013/01/30/john-kerry-bids-farewell-to-the-senate-after-28-years | title =John Kerry Bids Farewell to the Senate After 28 Years | first=Alex | last=Rogers | publisher=time.com | date =January 30, 2013}}</ref> Governor [[Deval Patrick]] appointed Democrat [[Mo Cowan]] to the seat.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.bloomberg.com/news/2013-01-30/patrick-appoints-longtime-donor-fulp-to-kerry-senate-seat.html | title =Patrick Appoints Former Staff Chief Cowan to Kerry Senate | first =Annie | last=Linskey | publisher=bloomberg.com | date =January 30, 2013}}</ref> Democratic Congressman [[Ed Markey]] beat Republican [[Gabriel E. Gomez]] in the [[United States Senate special election in Massachusetts, 2013|June 25, 2013 special election]] by a 55% to 45% margin.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.nytimes.com/2013/05/01/us/massachusetts-kerry-senate-replacement.html | title =Senior Congressman and Newcomer Win Senate Nods in Massachusetts | first =Katharine Q. | last=Seelye | publisher=nytimes.com | newspaper=[[The New York Times]] | date =April 30, 2013}}</ref> Markey will serve the remainder of Kerry's term, and is running for re-election in 2014.<ref name="markey1"/> Markey will be 68 years old in November 2014.

[[Hopkinton, Massachusetts|Hopkinton]] City Selectman Brian Herr is the Republican nominee.

=== Michigan ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Michigan, 2014}}
Six-term incumbent Senator and Chairman of the Armed Services Committee Democrat [[Carl Levin]], the longest serving Senator in Michigan's history, was re-elected with 63% of the vote in 2008. Levin announced on March 7, 2013 that he would not seek re-election.<ref name="freep1"/>

Three term Democratic Representative [[Gary Peters (Michigan politician)|Gary Peters]] of [[Michigan's 14th congressional district|MI-14]] is the Democratic nominee.<ref>{{cite news | last=Klug | first=Fritz | title=U.S. Senators Carl Levin, Debbie Stabenow endorse Gary Peters in 2014 U.S. Senate election | url=http://www.mlive.com/news/index.ssf/2013/05/us_senators_carl_levin_debbie.html | newspaper=M Live | date=May 23, 2013}}</ref>

Republican former Secretary of State [[Terri Lynn Land]] was unopposed for the Republican nomination.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.freep.com/article/20130603/NEWS15/306030050/Republican-Terri-Lynn-Land-joins-U-S-Senate-race | title =Republican Terri Lynn Land joins U.S. Senate race | first=Paul | last=Egan | publisher=[[Detroit Free Press]] | date =June 4, 2013}}</ref>

=== Minnesota ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Minnesota, 2014}}
One-term incumbent Democrat [[Al Franken]] unseated one-term Republican [[Norm Coleman]] by 312 votes in a contested three-way race with 42% of the vote in 2008; the third candidate in the race, [[Dean Barkley]] of the [[Independence Party of Minnesota]], won 15% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.minnpost.com/politics-policy/2009/04/judges%E2%80%99-%E2%80%98three-votes%E2%80%99-give-al-franken-convincing-win-senate-recount-trial | title=Judges’ ‘three votes’ give Al Franken convincing win in Senate recount trial | work=[[MinnPost]] | date=April 13, 2009 | accessdate=February 7, 2014 | last=Weiner | first=Jay}}</ref> Franken is seeking re-election.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://minnesota.publicradio.org/display/web/2012/08/15/politics/al-franken-fundraising | title=Franken creates potent fundraising machine in advance of re-election bid | work=[[Minnesota Public Radio]] | date=August 12, 2012 | accessdate=February 8, 2013 | last=Richert | first=Catharine}}</ref> State Representative [[Jim Abeler]],<ref name="abeler">{{cite web | url=http://minnesota.publicradio.org/display/web/2013/06/18/politics/abeler-senate | title=State Rep. Abeler running for US Senate | work=[[Minnesota Public Radio]] | date=June 18, 2013 | accessdate=June 18, 2013 | author=Pugmire, Tim and Tom Scheck}}</ref> [[St. Louis County, Minnesota|St. Louis County]] Commissioner Chris Dahlberg,<ref name="dahlberg">{{cite web|url=http://www.duluthnewstribune.com/event/article/id/275707/|title=St. Louis County commissioner announces run for U.S. Senate|date=August 20, 2013|accessdate=August 21, 2013|work=[[Duluth News Tribune]]}}</ref> co-CEO of Lazard Middle Market [[Mike McFadden]],<ref name="mcfadden" /> bison farmer and former hair salon owner Monti Moreno,<ref name="moreno">{{cite web | url=http://blogs.mprnews.org/capitol-view/2013/09/monti-moreno-enters-senate-race-without-musket/ | title=Monti Moreno enters Senate race, without musket | work=[[Minnesota Public Radio]] | date=September 12, 2013 | accessdate=February 3, 2014 | author=Zdechlik, Mark}}</ref> state Senator [[Julianne Ortman]],<ref name="ortman">{{cite web | url=http://www.startribune.com/politics/national/217857531.html | title=GOP state senator Ortman to announce plans for US Senate race at weekend event | work=[[Star Tribune]] | date=July 31, 2013 | accessdate=August 1, 2013}}</ref> and [[United States Navy Reserve|U.S. Navy reservist]] Phillip Parrish<ref name="parrish">{{cite web | url=http://ww3.postbulletin.com/news/stories/display.php?id=1547029 | title=GOP Senate candidates face-off in tea party forum | work=[[Post-Bulletin]] | date=January 17, 2014 | accessdate=February 7, 2014 | author=Carlson, Heather J.}}</ref> ran for the Republican nomination. McFadden won the Republican primary and is the Republican nominee in the general election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://wayback.archive.org/web/20140821090242/http://www.startribune.com/local/271008111.html|title=Businessman Mike McFadden wins Minnesota's GOP Senate primary; takes on Al Franken in November|publisher=|accessdate=October 27, 2014}}</ref>

Hannah Nicollet of the [[Independence Party of Minnesota]] is also running.<ref name="nicollet">{{cite web | url=http://www.mprnews.org/story/2014/02/06/hannah-nicollet-senate-bid | title=Ron Paul backer announces Minn. Senate bid as Independence Party candidate | work=[[Minnesota Public Radio]] | date=February 6, 2014 | accessdate=February 7, 2014 | author=Scheck, Tom}}</ref>

=== Mississippi ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Mississippi, 2014}}
Six-term incumbent Republican [[Thad Cochran]], re-elected with 62% of the vote in 2008, is running for re-election.<ref name=Running/> He will be 76 years old in 2014. Cochran was the last incumbent Senator to declare his plans, leading to widespread speculation that he would retire.<ref name=Guessing>{{cite news | title=Mississippi Senate race 2014: Guessing game over Thad Cochran run| url=http://www.politico.com/story/2013/08/mississippi-senate-race-thad-cochran-95350.html| work=Politico| date=August 8, 2013| accessdate=August 9, 2013}}</ref><ref name="Cochran unknown">{{cite web | url=http://www.politico.com/blogs/on-congress/2013/03/cochran-too-early-on-159825.html | title =Thad Cochran: Too early on 2014 | first=Manu | last=Raju | publisher=politico.com | date =May 20, 2013}}</ref>
[[Tea Party Movement|Tea Party]] candidate [[Chris McDaniel]], a conservative Mississippi state senator, ran against Cochran in the Republican primary.<ref name=McDaniel>{{cite news|last=Livingston|first=Abby|title=Tea Party Candidate Challenges Thad Cochran|url=http://atr.rollcall.com/tea-party-candidate-challenges-thad-cochran/|accessdate=October 17, 2013|newspaper=Roll Call|date=October 17, 2013}}</ref> Neither McDaniel nor Cochran was able to get 50% of the vote in the first round of the primary, so a [[runoff election]] was held June 24.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/05/us/politics/cochran-and-mcdaniel-mississippi-primary-runoff.html|title=Mississippi’s G.O.P. Senate Primary Headed to a Runoff|last=Martin|first=Jonathan|date=June 4, 2014|newspaper=New York Times|accessdate=June 4, 2014}}</ref>
Cochran won the runoff election by 51% to 49%.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://online.wsj.com/articles/thad-cochran-defeats-tea-party-backed-challenger-in-mississippi-gop-senate-runoff-1403666748|title=Thad Cochran Defeats Tea Party-Backed Challenger in Mississippi GOP Senate Runoff - WSJ|date=June 25, 2014|work=WSJ|accessdate=October 27, 2014}}</ref> McDaniel has mounted a legal challenge to the run-off, but the [[Chris McDaniel#Aftermath|challenge]] was unsuccessful at overturning the result.<ref name=GPender>{{cite news|last1=Pender|first1=Geoff|title=High court mulls McDaniel appeal|url=http://www.clarionledger.com/story/news/2014/10/02/high-court-mulls-mcdaniel-appeal/16601115/|accessdate=October 11, 2014|publisher=The Clarion-Ledger|date=October 2, 2014}}</ref>

Former Congressman [[Travis Childers]] is the Democratic nominee.<ref name=DJournal>{{cite news|last=Harrison|first=Bobby|title=Childers Running for Senate|url=http://djournal.com/news/childers-running-senate/|accessdate=February 28, 2014|newspaper=Daily Journal|date=February 28, 2014}}</ref>

=== Montana ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Montana, 2014}}
Six-term incumbent Democrat [[Max Baucus]], the longest serving Senator in Montana's history, was re-elected with 73% of the vote in 2008. Baucus announced on April 23, 2013 that he would retire in 2014, rather than seek re-election to a seventh term.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.npr.org/blogs/itsallpolitics/2013/04/23/178597359/montanas-max-baucus-to-retire-republicans-eye-2014-chances | title =Montana's Max Baucus To Retire; Republicans Eye 2014 Chances | first=Greg | last=Henderson | publisher=npr.org | date =April 23, 2013}}</ref> Baucus was appointed as the [[United States Ambassador to China]], leading him to resign from the Senate in February 2014.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/post-politics/wp/2014/02/06/max-baucus-confirmed-as-u-s-ambassador-to-china/ | title=Max Baucus confirmed as U.S. ambassador to China | first=Ed | last=O'Keefe | publisher=washingtonpost.com | date=February 6, 2014}}</ref>

Following Baucus's confirmation as ambassador, Governor [[Steve Bullock (Montana politician)|Steve Bullock]] appointed Lieutenant Governor [[John Walsh (U.S. politician)|John Walsh]] to fill the vacant senate seat.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2014/02/07/walsh-montana-senate-baucus-china/5278535/ | title=Montana gov taps John Walsh to replace Baucus in Senate | first=Catalina | last=Camia | publisher=usatoday.com | date=February 7, 2014}}</ref> Former Lieutenant Governor [[John Bohlinger]] was defeated by Walsh in the Democratic primary. Amid controversy over alleged plagiarism in a 2007 research paper, Walsh pulled out of the race.<ref>{{cite news | url= http://johnwalsh2014.com/ | title=Statement from U.S. Senator John Walsh}}</ref> The [[Montana Democratic Party]] held a special nominating convention on August 16 to choose a replacement for Walsh. First-term State Representative [[Amanda Curtis]] won the nomination, thereby becoming the new Democratic nominee.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://wayback.archive.org/web/20140816183336/http://www.sfgate.com/news/article/Rep-Amanda-Curtis-Women-s-rights-labor-activist-5693033.php|title=Rep. Amanda Curtis: Women's rights, labor activist|date=August 16, 2014|work=SFGate|accessdate=October 27, 2014}}</ref>

Congressman [[Steve Daines]] won the Republican nomination <ref>{{cite web|url=http://news.yahoo.com/montana-gop-congressman-announces-us-senate-bid-193717165--election.html|title=Montana GOP congressman announces US Senate bid|date=November 6, 2013|work=Yahoo News|accessdate=October 27, 2014}}</ref> over state Representative [[Champ Edmunds]] of [[Missoula, Montana|Missoula]] and David Leaser of [[Kalispell, Montana|Kalispell]].<ref>[http://www.washingtonpost.com/national/montana-democrats-get-higher-profile-us-senate-candidate-they-have-been-searching-for/2013/10/03/08eac8c8-2bf2-11e3-b141-298f46539716_story.html]{{Dead link|date=April 2014|bot=RjwilmsiBot}}</ref>

Since the adoption of the [[Seventeenth Amendment to the United States Constitution|17th Amendment]], this seat has been held exclusively by Democrats.

=== Nebraska ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Nebraska, 2014}}
One-term incumbent Republican [[Mike Johanns]] was elected with 58% of the vote in 2008. He is not seeking second term.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.ibtimes.com/sen-mike-johanns-nebraska-retire-2014-1092152 | title =Sen. Mike Johanns Of Nebraska To Retire In 2014 | first=Eric | last=Lipton | publisher=[[International Business Times]] | date =February 18, 2013}}</ref> Term limited Republican Governor [[Dave Heineman]] considered running for the Republican nomination, but ultimately decided against running.<ref>{{cite web | accessdate=May 29, 2013 | title= Neb. Gov. Heineman says no to Senate bid | first=Bernie | last=Becker | date=May 25, 2013 | url=http://thehill.com/blogs/ballot-box/senate-races/301989-nebraska-gov-heineman-says-no-to-senate-bid}}</ref> Former state Treasurer [[Shane Osborn]],<ref>{{cite web | url=http://journalstar.com/news/local/shane-osborn-enters-gop-senate-race/article_b758eb71-76d7-5e78-8e3a-6c36a1bccbf9.html | title =Shane Osborn enters GOP Senate race | first=Don | last=Walton | publisher=journalstar.com | date =June 2, 2013}}</ref> attorney Bart McLeay, banker Sid Dinsdale, and [[Midland University]] President [[Ben Sasse]] ran for the Republican nomination.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.omaha.com/article/20130701/NEWS/707029977/1694 | title =Attorney Bart McLeay to run for Nebraska U.S. Senate seat | author =Robynn Tysver | author2 =''[[Omaha World-Herald]]'' | publisher =omaha.com | date =}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.omaha.com/article/20130723/NEWS/130729545/1694#midland-university-president-sasse-files-for-senate | title =Midland University president Sasse files for Senate | author =''[[Associated Press]]'' | author2 =''[[Omaha World-Herald]]'' | publisher =omaha.com | date =July 23, 2013}}</ref> In the May 13 primary, Sasse won the Republican nomination.

[[Trial lawyer]] [[David Domina]] is the Democratic nominee.<ref>{{cite news | title=Dave Domina Wins Nebraska Senate Primary | url=http://www.huffingtonpost.com/2014/05/13/dave-domina-primary_n_5320260.html | publisher=The Huffington Post | date=May 13, 2014}}</ref>

=== New Hampshire ===
{{main|United States Senate election in New Hampshire, 2014}}
One-term incumbent Democrat [[Jeanne Shaheen]] was elected with 52% of the vote in 2008. She will be 67 years old in 2014. Shaheen is running for re-election.<ref name=Shaheen>{{cite web | url=http://www.fosters.com/apps/pbcs.dll/article?AID=/20120308/GJNEWS_01/703089867 | title=Dover NH, Rochester NH, Portsmouth NH, Laconia NH, Sanford ME | publisher=Fosters.com | date=March 8, 2012 | accessdate=November 24, 2012}}</ref>

[[Scott Brown]], who represented neighboring [[Massachusetts]] in the [[United States Senate|Senate]] from 2010 to 2012, is the Republican nominee.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.csmonitor.com/USA/Latest-News-Wires/2014/0909/Scott-Brown-wins-N.H.-primary-bid-for-US-Senate-video|title=Scott Brown wins N.H. primary bid for US Senate (+video)|author=Steven R. Hurst|date=September 9, 2014|work=The Christian Science Monitor|accessdate=October 27, 2014}}</ref>

=== New Jersey ===
{{main|United States Senate election in New Jersey, 2014}}
{{See also|United States Senate special election in New Jersey, 2013}}
Incumbent Democrat [[Frank Lautenberg]] was re-elected with 56% of the vote in 2008. After announcing he wouldn't seek re-election, Lautenberg died in June 2013.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/04/nyregion/frank-lautenberg-new-jersey-senator.html?pagewanted=all | title =Frank Lautenberg, New Jersey Senator in His 5th Term, Dies at 89 | first=Adam | last=Clymer | publisher=nytimes.com | newspaper=[[The New York Times]] | date =June 3, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2013/jun/03/frank-lautenberg-new-jersey-senator-dies | title =New Jersey senator Frank Lautenberg dies aged 89 | first=Matt | last=Williams | publisher=The Guardian | date =June 3, 2013 | location=London}}</ref> [[Mayors of Newark, New Jersey|Newark Mayor]] [[Cory Booker]], a Democrat, defeated Republican nominee [[Steve Lonegan]] by 55%-to-45% in a [[United States Senate special election in New Jersey, 2013|2013 special election]] to replace interim Republican appointee [[Jeffrey Chiesa]].<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.nytimes.com/projects/elections/2013/nj-special/senate/map.html?smid=tw-nytimes | work=The New York Times | title=New Jersey Senate - 2013 Election Results}}</ref> Booker is running for re-election in 2014. [[United States Senate elections, 1978|1978]] and [[United States Senate elections, 1982|1982]] Republican candidate and political operative [[Jeffrey Bell (political operative)|Jeffrey Bell]] is the Republican nominee.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.nysun.com/editorials/jeff-bell-for-senate/88738/|title=Jeff Bell for Senate|publisher=|accessdate=October 27, 2014}}</ref>

=== New Mexico ===
{{main|United States Senate election in New Mexico, 2014}}
One-term incumbent Democrat [[Tom Udall]] was elected with 61% of the vote in 2008. He will be 66 years old in 2014. Former [[Doña Ana County, New Mexico|Doña Ana County]] Republican Party Chairman David Clements and former New Mexico Republican Party Chairman [[Allen Weh]] sought the Republican nomination.<ref>[http://www.rollcall.com/news/dems_mission_in_new_mexico_breaking_steve_pearce-229057-1.html Democrats' Mission in N.M.: Breaking Steve Pearce : Roll Call Politics<!-- Bot generated title -->]</ref>  Weh won the June 3 primary and will face Udall in the general election.

=== North Carolina ===
{{main|United States Senate election in North Carolina, 2014}}
One-term incumbent Democrat [[Kay Hagan]] was elected with 53% of the vote against incumbent Republican [[Elizabeth Dole]] in 2008. Hagan will be 61 years old in 2014 and is seeking re-election.<ref>{{cite news | last=Sullivan | first=Sean | title=Sen. Kay Hagan says she will run for reelection in 2014 | url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/the-fix/wp/2012/11/27/sen-kay-hagan-says-she-will-run-for-reelection-in-2014 | publisher=washingtonpost.com | newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] | accessdate=February 8, 2013}}</ref><ref name="Hoffman">{{cite web | url=http://www.news-record.com/news/government/elections/article_23868bc8-d4ed-11e3-aa03-0017a43b2370.html | title=Results from Tuesday's primary elections" Read more: http://www.news-record.com/news/government/elections/article_23868bc8-d4ed-11e3-aa03-0017a43b2370.html | first=H. Scott | last=Hoffman | publisher=news-record.com | date=May 7, 2014 | accessdate=May 7, 2014}}</ref> State House Speaker [[Thom Tillis]] is the Republican nominatee.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.politico.com/story/2014/05/primary-results-north-carolina-ohio-indiana-106416.html?hp=l1 | title=Thom Tillis beats tea party challenge in North Carolina" Read more: http://www.politico.com/story/2014/05/primary-results-north-carolina-ohio-indiana-106416.html?hp=l1 | first=Tarini and Elizabeth | last=Parti and Titus | publisher=politico.com | date=May 6, 2014 | accessdate=May 7, 2014}}</ref> [[Sean Haugh]] won the Libertarian nomination.<ref name="Hoffman"/>

=== Oklahoma ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Oklahoma, 2014}}
Three-term incumbent Republican [[Jim Inhofe]] was re-elected with 57% of the vote in 2008. He will be 79 years old in 2014. Inhofe is seeking re-election. Matt Silverstein, an insurance agency owner, is running for the Democratic nomination.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.tulsaworld.com/article.aspx/DC_Report_Matt_Silverstein_files_notice_of_plan_to/20130630_11_A22_bSenat592022?rss_lnk=16|title=DC Report: Senate candidate|date=June 30, 2013|accessdate=July 11, 2013|work=Tulsa World|first=Randy|last=Krehbiel}}</ref>

=== Oklahoma (special) ===
{{main|United States Senate special election in Oklahoma, 2014}}
Two-term incumbent Republican [[Tom Coburn]] was re-elected with 71% of the vote in 2010, and was not scheduled to be up for election again until 2016. However, Coburn announced his intention to resign at the end of the [[113th United States Congress|113th Congress]]. A special election to fill his seat will take place in November 2014, concurrent with the other Senate elections.<ref name=OkSpecial>{{cite news|last=Cahn|first=Emily|title=Special Election Dates Set to Replace Coburn|url=http://atr.rollcall.com/oklahoma-gov-sets-dates-for-election-to-replace-coburn/|accessdate=January 17, 2014|newspaper=Roll Call|date=January 17, 2014}}</ref> Congressman [[James Lankford]] is the Republican nominee.<ref name=OkCan>{{cite news|last=Taylor|first=Jessica|title=Crowded primary likely in race for Coburn seat|url=http://thehill.com/blogs/ballot-box/senate-races/195783-crowded-primary-likely-in-race-for-coburn-seat|accessdate=January 17, 2014|newspaper=The Hill|date=January 17, 2014}}</ref><ref name="Oklahoma Farm Team">{{cite news|last=Cahn|first=Emily|title=In Oklahoma, Open Seats Could Come Sooner|url=http://www.rollcall.com/news/in_oklahoma_open_seats_could_come_sooner-230200-1.html?pg=2|accessdate=January 15, 2014|newspaper=Roll Call|date=January 15, 2014}}</ref> State Senator [[Constance N. Johnson|Connie Johnson]] is the Democratic nominee.<ref>{{cite news|last=Felder|first=Ben|title=Johnson announces Democratic bid for U.S. Senate |url=http://www.okgazette.com/oklahoma/article-20960-johnson-announces-de.html|accessdate=May 3, 2014|newspaper=Oklahoma Gazette|date=April 8, 2014}}</ref>

=== Oregon ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Oregon, 2014}}
One-term incumbent Democrat [[Jeff Merkley]] was narrowly elected with 49% of the vote in 2008. He will be 58 years old in 2014. Merkley is running for a second term. State representative [[Jason Conger]], attorney Tim Crawley, IT consultant Mark Callahan, neurosurgeon [[Monica Wehby]], and former [[Linn County, Oregon|Linn County]] Republican Chair Jo Rae Perkins all ran for the Republican nomination,<ref name=OPB>{{cite news|title=The Republican Challengers To Merkley And Kitzhaber|url=http://www.opb.org/radio/programs/thinkoutloud/segment/who-are-the-republican-challengers-to-merkley-and-kitzhaber/|accessdate=April 7, 2014|newspaper=OPB|date=January 27, 2014}}</ref> with Wehby ultimately winning the nomination in the May 20 primary.<ref name="yahoo1">{{cite web|url=http://news.yahoo.com/tea-party-shut-out-135533528.html |title=Is the party over? Tea party shut out in Tuesday's primaries - Yahoo News |publisher=News.yahoo.com |date=May 21, 2014 |accessdate=June 27, 2014}}</ref>

=== Rhode Island ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Rhode Island, 2014}}
Three-term incumbent Democrat [[Jack Reed (politician)|Jack Reed]] was re-elected with 73% of the vote in 2008. He will be 64 years old in 2014. Reed is running for re-election.<ref>[http://www.jackreed2014.com/ Jack Reed for United States Senate<!-- Bot generated title -->]</ref> Mark Zaccaria is the Republican nominee.

=== South Carolina ===
{{main|United States Senate election in South Carolina, 2014}}
Two-term incumbent Republican [[Lindsey Graham]] was re-elected with 58% of the vote in 2008. He will be 59 years old in 2014. Graham won the Republican nomination over a field that included state senator [[Lee Bright]]. State Senator [[Brad Hutto]] won the Democratic nomination.<ref>{{cite web | accessdate=June 10, 2014 | title=Brad Hutto wins Democratic nomination for US Senate seat| date=June 10, 2013 | publisher=WCSC | url=http://www.live5news.com/story/25732329/democrats-face-off-in-primary-for-us-senate-seat}}</ref>

=== South Carolina (special) ===
{{main|United States Senate special election in South Carolina, 2014}}
[[Jim DeMint]] was elected to a second term in 2010, but he resigned from the Senate in January 2013 to become president of [[The Heritage Foundation]], a conservative think-tank.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/national/south-carolina-republican-us-sen-jim-demint-resigning-to-take-over-at-heritage-foundation/2012/12/06/7a8433de-3fbc-11e2-8a5c-473797be602c_story.html | title= South Carolina Republican US Sen. Jim DeMint resigning to take over at Heritage Foundation | newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] | date=December 6, 2012 | accessdate=December 6, 2012 | deadurl=yes}} {{Dead link|date=April 2014|bot=RjwilmsiBot}}</ref> Governor [[Nikki Haley]] appointed [[United States House of Representatives|Congressman]] [[Tim Scott]] as DeMint's replacement.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.politico.com/story/2012/12/tim-scott-to-succeed-demint-in-senate-85169.html?hp=f1 | title=Tim Scott to succeed Jim DeMint in Senate | publisher=Politico.com | work=[[Politico]] | date=December 17, 2012 | accessdate=December 17, 2012}}</ref> Scott is the Republican nominee to serve the remainder of DeMint's term. Richland County Council member [[Joyce Dickerson]] won the Democratic nomination.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.wistv.com/story/25745002/dickerson-wins-dem-primary-to-face-scott-in-nov | title=Dickerson wins Dem primary, to face Scott in Nov. | publisher=wistv.com| date=June 10, 2014 | accessdate=June 10, 2014}}</ref>

=== South Dakota ===
{{main|United States Senate election in South Dakota, 2014}}
Three-term incumbent Democrat [[Tim Johnson (U.S. Senator)|Tim Johnson]] was re-elected with 63% of the vote in 2008. Johnson announced on March 26, 2013 that he would not run for reelection.<ref>{{cite news | url=http://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2013/03/25/tim-johnson-senate-retire-south-dakota/2018537 | title=Report: Democratic Sen. Johnson to retire | publisher=[[USA Today]] | date=March 25, 2013 | accessdate=March 25, 2013 | first1=Catalina | last1=Camia}}</ref> Former Congressional aide [[Rick Weiland]] is the Democratic nominee.<ref>{{cite web | accessdate=May 29, 2013 | title= South Dakota Dems’ primary fears ease in race to replace Sen. Johnson | first=Alexandra | last=Jaffe | date=May 12, 2013 | publisher=The Hill | url=http://thehill.com/blogs/ballot-box/senate-races/299151-senate-race-in-south-dakota-taking-shape}}</ref>

Among Republicans, former two-term Governor [[Mike Rounds]] announced his candidacy for the GOP nomination on November 29, 2012.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.mitchellrepublic.com/event/article/id/73011 | title=Mike Rounds announces 2014 U.S. Senate candidacy | publisher=The Daily Republic | date=November 29, 2012 | accessdate=November 29, 2012}}</ref> Rounds won the Republican nomination over state senator [[Larry Rhoden]], state representative [[Stace Nelson]], and physician Annette Bosworth.<ref>{{cite news|title=Stace Nelson to announce run for U.S. Senate|url=http://www.argusleader.com/article/20130813/UPDATES/130813001/Stace-Nelson-announce-run-U-S-Senate?gcheck=1|accessdate=August 16, 2013|newspaper=Argus Leader|date=August 13, 2013}}</ref>

Former Republican Senator [[Larry Pressler]] and Republican [[South Dakota Senate|State Senator]] [[Gordon Howie]] are running as [[independent (politics)|independents]].<ref>{{cite web|last=Cost |first=Jay |url=http://washingtonexaminer.com/former-south-dakota-sen.-larry-pressler-running-as-independent/article/2541242 |title=Former South Dakota Sen. Larry Pressler running as independent |publisher=WashingtonExaminer.com |date= |accessdate=June 27, 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.americanclarion.com/ensuring-choice-conservatives-29803 |title=Ensuring a Choice for South Dakota Conservatives &#124; American Clarion |publisher=Americanclarion.com |date= |accessdate=April 4, 2014}}</ref> Pressler has not committed to caucusing with either party, while Howie has said that he would caucus with the Senate Republicans.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/post-politics/wp/2014/10/10/meet-larry-pressler-the-one-man-band-shaking-up-the-battle-for-the-senate-majority/|accessdate=October 11, 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Five questions with Senate candidate Gordon Howie|url=http://rapidcityjournal.com/news/local/five-questions-with-senate-candidate-gordon-howie/article_dbd51854-2494-55be-b5bd-fed6eeb509a2.html|accessdate=October 11, 2014}}</ref>

=== Tennessee ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Tennessee, 2014}}
Two-term incumbent Republican [[Lamar Alexander]] was re-elected with 65% of the vote in 2008. He will be 74 years old in 2014. Alexander is seeking re-election to a third term.<ref name=alexander>{{cite web | url=http://www.politico.com/news/stories/0911/63919_Page2.html | title=Alexander quitting leadership post in Senate | publisher=Politico.com | work=[[Politico]] | date=September 20, 2011}}</ref>

On August 7, 2014, Alexander won the Republican nomination over six challengers, including State Representative [[Joe Carr (Tennessee politician)|Joe Carr]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://abcnews.go.com/blogs/politics/2014/08/tennessee-sen-lamar-alexander-defeats-tea-party-challenger-joe-carr/|title=Tennessee Sen. Lamar Alexander Defeats Tea Party Challenger Joe Carr|author=ABC News|work=ABC News Blogs|accessdate=October 27, 2014}}</ref>

On November 4, 2014, Alexander will face Democratic nominee Gordon Ball, [[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian Party]] nominee Joshua James,<ref name="tennessee.gov">{{cite web|url=http://www.tennessee.gov/sos/election/PetitionsFiled.pdf|title=Petitions Filed for Governor, United States Senate, and United States House of Representatives}}</ref> [[Constitution Party (United States)|Constitution Party]] nominee Joe Wilmothm,<ref name="tennessee.gov"/> and independent Danny Page<ref name="tennessee.gov"/> in the general election.

=== Texas ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Texas, 2014}}
Two-term incumbent Republican [[John Cornyn]], the [[Senate Minority Whip]], was re-elected with 55% of the vote in 2008. Cornyn is seeking re-election, and won the 2014 Republican primary with 59% of the vote.

Dentist [[David Alameel]] and Kesha Rogers, a volunteer for The [[Lyndon LaRouche]] Policy Institute, faced each other in a run-off election for the Democratic nomination.<ref name=TXDem>{{cite news|last=Gillman|first=Todd J.|title=Dallas dentist David Alameel brings big fortune to U.S. Senate race|url=http://www.dallasnews.com/news/politics/headlines/20131202-dallas-dentist-david-alameel-brings-big-fortune-to-u.s.-senate-race.ece|accessdate=December 10, 2013|newspaper=The Dallas Morning News|date=December 2, 2013}}</ref> Alameel won the run-off and is the Democratic nominee.<ref>{{cite web|last=Associated |first=The |url=http://www.khou.com/news/Texas-Democrats-nominate-Alameel-for-US-Senate--260857831.html |title=Texas Democrats nominate Alameel for US Senate &#124; khou.com Houston |publisher=Khou.com |date=May 27, 2014 |accessdate=June 27, 2014}}</ref>

=== Virginia ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Virginia, 2014}}
One-term incumbent Democrat [[Mark Warner]] was elected with 65% of the vote in 2008; he is seeking re-election. He will be 59 years old in 2014. [[Ed Gillespie]], former [[Republican National Committee|RNC]] Chairman and presidential adviser, is running for the Republican nomination. [[Robert Sarvis]], the Libertarian nominee for [[Governor of Virginia|Governor]] in [[Virginia gubernatorial election, 2013|2013]], is also running.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.politico.com/story/2014/01/virginia-senate-election-2014-libertarian-102830.html?hp=r3 |title=Libertarian to run for Senate in Va. |last1=Schultheis |first1=Emily |date=January 29, 2014 |website=politico.com |accessdate=January 30, 2014}}</ref>

=== West Virginia ===
{{main|United States Senate election in West Virginia, 2014}}
Five-term incumbent Democrat [[Jay Rockefeller]] was re-elected with 64% of the vote in 2008. He announced on January 11, 2013 that he would not seek reelection to a sixth term.<ref name="West Virginia Democratic Sen Jay Rockefeller won’t run in 2014"/> Secretary of State [[Natalie Tennant]] won the Democratic nomination.<ref name="Tennant Running">{{cite news|last=Livingston|first=Abby|title=Democrats Land Recruit for West Virginia Senate Race {{!}} #WVSEN|url=http://atr.rollcall.com/democrats-land-recruit-for-west-virginia-senate-race/|accessdate=September 14, 2013|newspaper=Roll Call|date=September 13, 2013}}</ref>

On November 26, 2012, Republican Congresswoman [[Shelley Moore Capito]] announced her plans to run for the seat, in hopes of becoming the first Republican Senator elected from West Virginia since 1956.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.politico.com/news/stories/1112/84211.html?hp=f3 | title=Shelley Moore Capito makes Senate bid vs. Jay Rockefeller official – David Catanese | publisher=Politico.com | work=[[Politico]] | date= | accessdate=December 3, 2012}}</ref> Moore Capito won the nomination.

=== Wyoming ===
{{main|United States Senate election in Wyoming, 2014}}
Three-term incumbent Republican [[Mike Enzi]] was re-elected with 76% of the vote in 2008. He will be 70 years old in 2014. Enzi is seeking re-election. [[Liz Cheney]], daughter of former Vice President [[Dick Cheney]], briefly entered the race for the Republican nomination, but dropped her bid in January 2014.<ref name=theguardian>{{cite web | url=http://www.theguardian.com/world/2014/jan/06/liz-cheney-ends-senate-campaign-health-family | title=Liz Cheney ends Senate campaign over 'serious health issues' in family | publisher=The Guardian | date=January 6, 2014 | accessdate=January 9, 2014}}</ref>
On August 19, Mike Enzi won the Republican primary election with 82% of the vote, and Democrat Charlie Hardy won his party's primary election with 48% of the vote.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://hosted.ap.org/dynamic/files/elections/2014/by_state/WY_US_Senate_0819.html?SITE=AP&SECTION=POLITICS| title =Wyoming - Summary Vote Results| author = ASSOCIATED PRESS| publisher =hosted.ap.org| date =June 3, 2013}}</ref>

